      "answer_text": "Use `hermes-all-three`. It is the composed engine, and it is the only package that runs A, B and D together on Hermes.\n\n**What stops three separate plugins from being active at once** is the single-select rule, from `hermes-plugins/CONTRACT.md` §3. `PluginRegistration.register_context_engine` (`hermes_cli/plugins.py:717`) refuses a second engine. It checks `if self._manager._context_engine is not None: logger.warning(...); return`. The first engine to register wins, and the later ones are skipped with a warning.\n\nSelection works the same way. `context.engine: <name>` in `config.yaml` picks exactly one engine, and the host holds it as `agent.context_compressor`. The ABC's hooks (`select_context`, `on_turn_complete`, `get_tool_schemas`, `handle_tool_call`) all belong to that one instance. So the combination has to live inside one engine.\n\nI'm going from my earlier reading of `CONTRACT.md`, the Hermes README section and the folder listing. I never opened `hermes-all-three/src/.../engine.py`, so I can't say how it orders or wires the three practices internally. The line number `:717` comes from the pinned clone of `hermes-agent`, which isn't in this repo.\n\nThe Claude Code port is different. Claude Code runs the hooks of every enabled plugin, so the three single plugins could technically coexist. I made `claude-code-all-three` for convenience, not out of necessity. Installing it next to the single ones would still run each hook twice."
    },
